Definition. $1$ is a natural number defined as the successor of $0$.

Definition. $2$ is a natural number defined as the successor of $1$.

Definition. Let $x \in N$. Then we can say that $x$ is even.

Definition. Let $x \in N$. Then we can say that $x$ is odd.

Even0. $0$ is even.

EvenSucc. Let $n$ be an odd natural number. Then the successor of $n$ is even.

OddSucc. Let $n$ be an even natural number. Then the successor of $n$ is odd.

Prop1. $1$ is odd. Proof. By even0. By the third axiom of parity, the successor of $0$ is odd.

Prop2. Let $n \in N$. Then $n$ is even, if and only if $n$ is divisible by $2$.

Prop3. Every natural number is even or odd. Proof. We reason by induction: let $x$ be $0$. By even0. A fortiori $0$ is even or $0$ is odd. Let $x$ be a natural number. Assume that $x$ is even or odd. ( y) by y, $x$ is even or odd. We reason by cases: \item assume that $x$ is even. ( h) by h, $x$ is even. By the third axiom of parity, the successor of $x$ is odd. A fortiori the successor of $x$ is even or the successor of $x$ is odd. \item assume that $x$ is odd. ( h) by h, $x$ is odd. By the second axiom of parity, the successor of $x$ is even. A fortiori the successor of $x$ is even or the successor of $x$ is odd. Hence the successor of $x$ is even or odd. We conclude that for all natural numbers $x$, $x$ is even or odd.
